[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding OWASP Top 10 Vulnerabilities and Mitigation"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"The OWASP Top 10 is a standard awareness document that represents a broad consensus on the most critical security risks to web applications. Understanding these vulnerabilities and learning how to mitigate them is essential for any cybersecurity professional or software developer."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Injection (A03:2021)"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Injection flaws, such as SQL, NoSQL, OS, and LDAP injection, occur when untrusted data is sent to an interpreter as part of a command or query. The attacker's hostile data can trick the interpreter into executing unintended commands or accessing data without proper authorization."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Impact: Data loss, corruption, lack of accountability, or complete host takeover."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Mitigation: Use safe APIs, implement parameterized queries, and use Object-Relational Mapping (ORM) frameworks."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"String query = "SELECT * FROM accounts WHERE custID = ?";
PreparedStatement pstmt = connection.prepareStatement(query);
pstmt.setString(1, customerId);
ResultSet results = pstmt.executeQuery();"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Broken Authentication (A07:2021)"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Application functions related to authentication and session management are often implemented incorrectly, allowing attackers to compromise passwords, keys, or session tokens to assume other users' identities."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Impact: Unauthorized access to user accounts, administrative privileges, and sensitive data."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Mitigation: Implement multi-factor authentication (MFA), enforce strong password policies, and use secure session management with proper timeouts."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Cross-Site Scripting - XSS (A03:2021)"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"XSS flaws occur whenever an application includes untrusted data in a web page without proper validation or escaping, allowing attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Impact: Session hijacking, redirection to malicious sites, and defacement of web pages."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Mitigation: Context-aware output encoding and implementing a strict Content Security Policy (CSP)."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' https://trustedscripts.com;"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Securing web applications requires a proactive approach. By integrating security testing early in the Software Development Life Cycle (SDLC) and adhering to the mitigation strategies outlined by OWASP, organizations can significantly reduce their attack surface."}]
